WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.300
If IKEA is the global face of&nbsp;
Sweden, Walmart might be America’s.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.760
But despite, apparently, being one of&nbsp;
the country’s “most patriotic” brands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.260
over half of all Walmarts are&nbsp;
located outside the United States.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:25.620
Its 10,500 stores are scattered across 24&nbsp;
countries, 46 brands, and 4 continents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.680
from Botswana and El Salvador to the&nbsp;
strange and exotic lands of Ohio.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.140
Target, on the other hand,&nbsp;
operates in just one country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:44.220
In its entire 120-year history, it&nbsp;
only once tried to expand overseas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.380
In a mere two years, Target opened&nbsp;
over a hundred stores in Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:56.340
failed spectacularly, and became a textbook,&nbsp;
seven billion dollar example of what not to do.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.040
It was such a disaster, in fact,&nbsp;
that someone wrote an actual two-act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.620
play re-enacting the events. It was even&nbsp;
performed in one of the abandoned stores.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:15.300
Sponsored by Nebula, the platform&nbsp;
created, owned, and loved by creators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.880
Sign up for exclusive, early, and&nbsp;
bonus content from PolyMatter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.820
hundreds of other channels&nbsp;
you’re guaranteed to love.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:31.320
The Greek Tragedy known as Target Canada&nbsp;
begins with a company called “Zellers”.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.640
Non-Canadians can think of Zellers like a colder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.040
more polite version of Kmart — a large,&nbsp;
national chain of discount department stores.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:49.980
And like Kmart, it was, by the early 2010s,&nbsp;
more of a 90s time capsule than an actual store.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.120
But — to its credit — rather than merely watch as&nbsp;
its brand slowly aged into inevitable bankruptcy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.240
like a certain company whose&nbsp;
name rhymes with “Spears”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.180
it threw in the towel and sold its&nbsp;
only asset that still had value: land.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:13.080
The Zellers name may not have meant much,&nbsp;
but its nearly 200 leases certainly did.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.540
Meanwhile, Target was rapidly&nbsp;
approaching saturation in America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:27.000
After nearly two decades of 8% average growth,&nbsp;
only 10 new stores were built in all of 2010.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.120
And where better to expand to than its friendly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.760
northern neighbor — the one just 225 miles&nbsp;
from Target’s corporate headquarters?

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.580
Besides the obvious cultural and&nbsp;
linguistic overlap, about 300,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.300
people cross what is the longest&nbsp;
border in the world each day.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.100
The company estimated that one in&nbsp;
every ten Canadians had shopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.380
at an American Target during the previous year.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.180
Besides, many U.S. corporations&nbsp;
have done well up north.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.980
Of Canada’s top 10 retailers&nbsp;
by revenue, five are American.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.080
This was not, all of this is to say, a crazy idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.460
With similar demographics,&nbsp;
pre-existing brand awareness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.860
and relatively few cultural differences,&nbsp;
Target Canada had everything going for it.

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.240
The only thing holding it back&nbsp;
was the size of its stores.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:28.800
There just aren’t that many 1-200,000 square&nbsp;
foot buildings with Pentagon-sized parking lots.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.180
Then along came Zellers. This was the&nbsp;
opportunity Target had been waiting for.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:45.240
In 2011, Target paid $1.8 billion Canadian&nbsp;
dollars to buy 189 Zellers stores, 54 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:51.120
which it immediately re-sold to other companies,&nbsp;
including none other than its good friend Walmart.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:04:00.300
There was, however, one major catch. From the&nbsp;
moment the deal was signed, the clock was ticking.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.240
Not only was every day spent cleaning,&nbsp;
remodeling, and renovating these stores a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.180
day it wasn’t making money, but Target bought&nbsp;
only the leases, not the land, outright.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.240
They still had to deal with landlords.&nbsp;
Landlords who weren’t too keen on letting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.820
these huge buildings sit empty — or worse —&nbsp;
bustling with construction for years on end.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:31.980
To avoid deterring shoppers from other stores in&nbsp;
the area, it all had to happen fast. Really fast.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.740
Bringing these retail museums into the&nbsp;
21st century required more than a fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.900
coat of paint. About $10-11 million&nbsp;
worth of work for each location.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.240
Still, Target trudged ahead on&nbsp;
what it called “Project Bacon”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.920
starting with the launch of three&nbsp;
Ontario stores in March of 2013.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.700
Their signs, which read “We’re open (mostly)”&nbsp;
hinted at the disaster soon to come.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:05.700
For the next 10 months, Target opened one&nbsp;
new Canadian store every 2 days on average.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:12.120
It was the quickest expansion in company&nbsp;
history — a feat that continued through 2014.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.840
But first, Target had a serious problem.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.080
It wasn’t a lack of demand — Canadians&nbsp;
were eager to give the company a chance.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:29.820
The problem was a lack of inventory.&nbsp;
Shelf after shelf sat totally empty.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.640
One week, almost every item featured on&nbsp;
Target’s promotional flier was out of stock.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.400
With nothing to buy, it’s no wonder&nbsp;
Target’s Canadian stores sold far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.920
less merchandise than their American counterparts.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.900
It all traced back to a single&nbsp;
decision made months earlier.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.820
And to understand why, we’ll need to&nbsp;
take a brief detour into everyone’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:58.800
favorite dinner table topic — the&nbsp;
wonderful world of enterprise software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.120
And it’s only right that you hear&nbsp;
this in the form of a PowerPoint…

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.800
Huge, billion-dollar mega-corporations such as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.300
for example, e.g. Target are simply too&nbsp;
big for any one person to keep track of.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:20.220
So, naturally, they turn to… computers! More&nbsp;
specifically, something called ERP — Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:26.100
Resource Planning. You can think of ERP&nbsp;
like the mother of all excel spreadsheets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.300
For the kids, an all-knowing,&nbsp;
all-seeing Notion dashboard.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.180
Your company’s ERP system knows how many&nbsp;
fractions of a cent it costs to ship this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:41.340
lamp from distribution center to store, how&nbsp;
many different colors this couch comes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.460
and at what exact point to re-order more dark-blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.260
oversized throw blankets based on the time&nbsp;
of year, recent sales, and diesel prices.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:54.360
Simply put: ERP is the brain&nbsp;
of a giant corporation.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:01.500
Target already had its own, custom&nbsp;
ERP built for America — but it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:05.820
designed to work only in English,&nbsp;
with U.S. Dollars, inches, and feet.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.020
So, rather than try to&nbsp;
jury-rig its American system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:15.000
Target opted to start fresh,&nbsp;
to buy a new ERP called SAP.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.200
Spoiler alert: this was a terrible idea.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.800
Now, back to your regular scheduled programming…

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.960
To Target executives, this sounded&nbsp;
like the best, most sensible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:33.960
long-term decision. Better to rip the band-aid off&nbsp;
quickly by switching to the software used by all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:39.540
the Fortune 500 companies, the one trusted&nbsp;
by Disney, and Burger King, and Coca-Cola.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:46.200
To anyone in the tech industry, however,&nbsp;
this was an obvious recipe for disaster.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.240
The first thing to understand&nbsp;
about SAP is how it makes money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.420
Like many enterprise software companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:59.160
it’s just as much in the consulting business&nbsp;
as it is in the writing software business.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.300
In fact, providing support can be so profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.500
that it’s not unheard of for companies&nbsp;
to give away their software for free.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.620
This, for instance, is SAP’s third&nbsp;
quarter 2022 investor report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.620
You can see that at least 38% of its&nbsp;
revenue came from “software support”.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.740
Anyone who's ever had the misfortune of&nbsp;
using enterprise software knows the secret:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:31.980
when the people — executives — who buy the&nbsp;
software don’t themselves use it, it sucks.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:39.000
But because SAP also makes a huge chunk of&nbsp;
its money from support, it’s even worse.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.440
How much incentive do they really&nbsp;
have to make it easy-to-use?

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:52.080
Now, the point is not that SAP doesn’t have its&nbsp;
uses. There’s a reason so many companies buy it.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.600
When ERP systems work, they can do&nbsp;
things no human could possibly do.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.520
And if SAP weren’t dependable,&nbsp;
Disney wouldn’t be using them.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:10.920
But for them to be dependable, the data you feed&nbsp;
them has to be perfect. Garbage in, garbage out.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:17.100
And by “data”, we’re talking about the width,&nbsp;
height, length, color, weight, quantity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:23.820
description, price, and so on for every last&nbsp;
one of the 75,000 unique items sold by Target.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.440
Now add French, an entirely&nbsp;
different system of measurement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:34.500
and a new currency to the mix — not to mention&nbsp;
the difficulty of learning to use SAP — and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.900
can see how this was fundamentally incompatible&nbsp;
with Target Canada’s extremely tight deadline.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.460
Software, famously, is finished when it’s&nbsp;
finished. Even on the best of days, deadlines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.520
schedules, and road-maps&nbsp;
are just wishful thinking.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.820
Implementing an ERP is no different.&nbsp;
However long you think it might take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:02.280
add a couple years, and, congratulations,&nbsp;
you’ve discovered the best case scenario.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.220
Sobeys, Canada’s second-largest&nbsp;
supermarket, gave up on SAP despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:13.980
spending 4 years and $89 million after&nbsp;
its databases were shut down for 5 days.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:19.080
Another company’s transition took&nbsp;
two years longer than expected.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:23.940
So, you can probably guess what&nbsp;
happened when Target Canada tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:29.280
to do the whole thing in two years…&nbsp;
complete, unmitigated disaster.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.040
While its store shelves sat empty,&nbsp;
products piled up in distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.800
centers. Target had all the inventory&nbsp;
it needed — it just didn’t know where.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:45.300
Eventually, the company was forced&nbsp;
to rent extra storage space.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:53.160
It soon discovered the culprit — a mere 30% of the&nbsp;
information entered into the system was accurate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:59.040
The other 70% might be measured in&nbsp;
inches, misspelled, or simply missing.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.260
Faced with no other option, the&nbsp;
entire Target merchandising team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:09.960
spent days manually verifying the&nbsp;
information for all 75,000 products.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.580
On top of this, analysts, whose&nbsp;
performance was measured based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.540
on their ability to keep products in&nbsp;
stock, had completely turned off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.780
automatic replenishment system&nbsp;
so they wouldn’t be penalized.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:27.420
Target wasn’t just flying blind — its&nbsp;
gauges were feeding it false information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:32.580
How could it possibly take-off&nbsp;
successfully under those conditions?

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:41.640
Now, as bad as this was — and, it was bad,&nbsp;
it probably wasn’t fatal. Supply chains are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:46.680
just plain hard and it always takes time for&nbsp;
companies to acclimate to a new environment.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:53.160
Nor was it any big scandal that Target’s&nbsp;
prices were about 15% higher in Canada.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:58.260
The country is about 9 times less&nbsp;
densely populated than the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:01.980
which makes it much more expensive&nbsp;
to get products into stores.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:08.160
Every last item for all 133&nbsp;
stores was sourced from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:13.560
of just three distribution centers&nbsp;
— in Milton, Balzac, and Cornwall.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:19.080
The latter, therefore, had to ship&nbsp;
products as far away as St. John’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:24.540
Newfoundland — a 1,600-mile,&nbsp;
33-hour drive and ferry ride away.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.860
Not to mention: fuel is more expensive in Canada.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:33.900
But for prices to be visibly&nbsp;
higher and for shelves to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.080
routinely empty was simply a stretch too far.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.860
By 2014, just 18% of surveyed Canadians said&nbsp;
they were “very satisfied” with the company.

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:48.900
Above all, though, the true&nbsp;
fatal flaw in Target’s plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:52.620
the real root of its problems,&nbsp;
was the deal with Zellers.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:59.100
Had it tested the market with one or two stores&nbsp;
to begin with, like J Crew and Nordstrom did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.720
it would’ve had the necessary focus to&nbsp;
correct any speed bumps and learn from them.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:11.880
Instead, sparks soon turned into a 133-store&nbsp;
forest fire. It was too much, too fast.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.040
It also didn’t help that many of these&nbsp;
stores were in sub-par locations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:24.540
When it entered Canada, Target wasn’t “Tar-jey”,&nbsp;
it was a fresh coat of paint over a former Kmart.

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:29.700
All the shiny bright red spheres in&nbsp;
the world couldn’t make up for the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:34.560
these buildings were situated in run-down&nbsp;
strip malls and C-Class shopping centers.

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:42.300
The company lost $940 million in the first year&nbsp;
alone, and the problem only compounded from there.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:48.540
So, after a widely-publicized data breach,&nbsp;
followed by a couple of lackluster holiday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:54.240
seasons — the bread-and-butter of retail&nbsp;
— Target Canada looked for an exit.

00:13:55.140 --> 00:14:00.960
And on April 1st, 2015 — just&nbsp;
757 days after the first store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:04.140
opened — Target announced it was leaving Canada.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.580
Despite spending $7 billion, hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:16.140
17,000 employees, and taking over 133&nbsp;
stores, it left with hardly a trace.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:23.460
This was the Zellers’ liquidation sale in&nbsp;
2011 and this was the Target liquidation sale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:27.720
possibly at the very same location,&nbsp;
just a few short years later.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:33.240
One store in Ottawa was completely&nbsp;
finished but unopened at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:39.420
The pristine, untouched Target was sold&nbsp;
to Walmart, along with dozens of others.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:45.300
And Canadians, 75% of whom live&nbsp;
less than 100 miles from America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:48.480
quietly went back to crossing the border.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:54.480
It was as if one of the biggest disasters&nbsp;
in retail history never happened.

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:59.760
One thing that, I hope, will leave&nbsp;
an impression is my video debunking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:03.120
the myth that China “lifted 800&nbsp;
million people out of poverty”.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:06.660
This persistent myth has been&nbsp;
used as a sort of “get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:10.920
jail free card” by the authorities&nbsp;
in Beijing. There’s a dark history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:15.180
behind this claim — one that would&nbsp;
surely get demonetized on YouTube.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:21.300
And that’s precisely why we created&nbsp;
Nebula, the streaming platform built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:28.020
